1 OVMS_AGENT
 OVMS Agent command and settings reference.

 This HELP source documents the current interactive command surface and the
 persistent SETTINGS choices.  Each entry explains what the feature does and
 when or why you would normally use it.

 The source is intended to be loaded into an OpenVMS HELP library with the
 OpenVMS Librarian.  M282 acceptance validates the exact Librarian syntax and
 representative lookups on the canonical VAX system.

 Additional information available:
  INTRODUCTION   How to choose among the major command families.
  SETTINGS       Persistent configuration and legacy logical overrides.
  COMMANDS       Complete interactive command reference.
  SAFETY         Choosing read-only, workspace, full, and bounded automation.

2 INTRODUCTION
 OVMS Agent has overlapping ways to inspect, plan, change, build, and operate a
 project.  Choose the narrowest command that fits the task.

 For ordinary inspection, use project commands such as READ, GREP, STATUS,
 GITSTATUS, GITDIFF, and the symbol-analysis commands.  For AI-assisted work
 without changes, use ASK, CHAT, REVIEW, AGENT, or AGENT/PLAN.  For controlled
 writes, use AGENT/WRITE, a structured refactoring command, CHANGESET, or an
 approved saved-plan execution path.  Use DCL only when a direct OpenVMS
 command is actually required and the full approval and execution gates are
 intentionally enabled.

 Persistent operational configuration belongs in SETTINGS.  Legacy
 OVMS_AGENT_* logical names remain compatible and, when defined, override the
 corresponding saved setting.

2 SAFETY
 Use the least-permissive mechanism that can complete the task.

 read-only approval
   Best for inspection, planning, explanations, searches, and diagnostics when
   no project file should change.

 workspace approval
   Best when a planned operation needs controlled changes inside the approved
   project workspace.  It does not imply unrestricted DCL or external writes.

 full approval
   Use only when an operation deliberately requires the highest normal policy
   tier, such as DCL or another explicitly FULL-gated operation.

 Guarded writes and DCL execution are separate SETTINGS gates.  Enabling a gate
 does not bypass approval policy.  SETTINGS identifies a legacy logical
 override so you can tell when a saved value is not the effective value.

 AGENT/AUTOPILOT has a hard safety floor.  It is limited to bounded local
 workspace repair/build behavior and never turns FULL-gated external work or
 arbitrary DCL into automatic work.

2 SETTINGS
 Command forms:
   SETTINGS
   SETTINGS SHOW
   SETTINGS GET name
   SETTINGS SET name value
   SETTINGS TOGGLE guarded_writes|dcl_execution
   SETTINGS RESET
   SETTINGS RELOAD

 Use SETTINGS with no arguments for the numbered interactive menu.  Use SHOW
 for a noninteractive snapshot, GET when troubleshooting or scripts need one
 value, SET for an explicit saved value, TOGGLE for the two Boolean execution
 gates, RESET to restore saved defaults, and RELOAD after the settings file was
 changed outside the current process.

 Saved settings normally come from SYS$LOGIN:OVMS_AGENT_SETTINGS.DAT.  A
 defined legacy logical takes precedence over its saved value.  SETTINGS shows
 the effective source and warns when a saved value is changed while a legacy
 logical still overrides it.

 Interactive menu:
   1  guarded_writes
   2  dcl_execution
   3  provider
   4  model
   5  max_output_tokens
   6  approval_policy
   7  net_allow
   8  net_deny
   9  auto_turns
  10  auto_writes

 Interactive menu commands:
   R or RELOAD    Reload saved settings.
   D or DEFAULTS  Restore saved defaults after confirmation.
   Q or QUIT      Leave the SETTINGS menu.

 SETTINGS RESET does not delete provider profiles and does not change the
 selected model.

3 GUARDED_WRITES
 Setting: guarded_writes
 Aliases: writes, write
 Legacy logical: OVMS_AGENT_WRITE_ENABLED
 Default: OFF

 Performs:
   Enables the project write gate used by guarded file-changing workflows.

 Use when:
   Turn it ON when you intentionally want approved workspace changes.  Keep it
   OFF during audits, exploration, review, or any session where accidental file
   modification should be impossible.

 Accepted SET values include ON/OFF, YES/NO, TRUE/FALSE, and 1/0.

3 DCL_EXECUTION
 Setting: dcl_execution
 Alias: dcl
 Legacy logical: OVMS_AGENT_DCL_ENABLED
 Default: OFF

 Performs:
   Enables the DCL execution gate.  DCL still requires its approval and write
   prerequisites.

 Use when:
   Turn it ON only when the task genuinely needs an OpenVMS command to execute.
   Leave it OFF for ordinary source inspection, planning, Git status, and
   project editing because narrower dedicated commands exist for those jobs.

 Accepted SET values include ON/OFF, YES/NO, TRUE/FALSE, and 1/0.

3 PROVIDER
 Setting: provider
 Alias: provider_profile

 Performs:
   Selects the active persistent AI provider profile.

 Use when:
   Change it when moving between providers, endpoints, or accounts represented
   by different saved profiles.  Use PROVIDER ADD first if the desired profile
   does not exist.

 Provider profiles are managed separately from ordinary SETTINGS defaults.

3 MODEL
 Setting: model

 Performs:
   Selects the model name for the active provider profile.

 Use when:
   Change it when the same provider offers multiple compatible models and you
   want a different capability, latency, context, or cost tradeoff without
   creating another provider profile.

 The selected model is not reset by SETTINGS RESET.

3 MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS
 Setting: max_output_tokens
 Aliases: output_tokens, tokens
 Legacy logical: OVMS_AGENT_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS
 Default: 2048
 Range: 64 through 32768

 Performs:
   Caps the requested LLM output token budget.

 Use when:
   Raise it for long plans, reviews, or explanations that are being cut off.
   Lower it when concise output, lower provider usage, or shorter latency is
   more important.

3 APPROVAL_POLICY
 Setting: approval_policy
 Alias: approval
 Legacy logical: OVMS_AGENT_APPROVAL_POLICY
 Default: read-only
 Values: read-only, workspace, full

 Performs:
   Sets the normal session approval tier used by guarded agent operations.

 Use when:
   Choose read-only for analysis and planning, workspace for intentional
   controlled project edits, and full only when a command explicitly requires
   the highest policy tier.  Do not use full merely to make routine commands
   more convenient.

3 NET_ALLOW
 Setting: net_allow
 Alias: network_allow
 Legacy logical: OVMS_AGENT_NET_ALLOW
 Default: empty

 Performs:
   Stores the allowed-domain policy text used by network-aware guarded work.

 Use when:
   Outbound access must be restricted to an explicit set of approved domains.
   Enter NONE through SETTINGS to clear the saved value.

3 NET_DENY
 Setting: net_deny
 Alias: network_deny
 Legacy logical: OVMS_AGENT_NET_DENY
 Default: empty

 Performs:
   Stores the denied-domain policy text used by network-aware guarded work.

 Use when:
   Specific hosts or domains must remain inaccessible even when broader network
   access is otherwise permitted.  Enter NONE to clear the saved value.

3 AUTO_TURNS
 Setting: auto_turns
 Aliases: autonomous_turns, agent_turns
 Legacy logical: OVMS_AGENT_AUTO_TURNS
 Default: 12
 Range: 1 through 32

 Performs:
   Limits autonomous model/tool turns in one bounded autonomous run.

 Use when:
   Lower it for small tasks or earlier human review.  Raise it for legitimate
   multi-step local work that cannot finish within the default turn budget.

 Saved changes apply to the next autonomous run without restarting OVMS Agent.
 A legacy logical override wins over the saved value.  Regression-test-only
 overrides remain higher precedence inside tests.

3 AUTO_WRITES
 Setting: auto_writes
 Aliases: autonomous_writes, write_actions
 Legacy logical: OVMS_AGENT_AUTO_WRITES
 Default: 3
 Range: 1 through 8

 Performs:
   Limits autonomous write actions in one bounded autonomous run.

 Use when:
   Keep it low for narrowly scoped repairs.  Raise it only when a planned local
   task legitimately requires several distinct guarded writes and you still
   want a hard stop before broader change.

 Saved changes apply to the next autonomous run without restarting OVMS Agent.
 A legacy logical override wins over the saved value.  Regression-test-only
 overrides remain higher precedence inside tests.

2 COMMANDS
 The command topics are grouped by purpose.  Slash commands are written
 literally in each entry; HELP topic keys use underscores for simple OpenVMS
 HELP navigation.

 Available command groups:
   CORE        General commands, settings, profiles, DCL, and change sets.
   PROJECT     Repository and file inspection/edit commands.
   SYMBOLS     C symbol analysis and structured refactoring.
   LLM         ASK, CHAT, REVIEW, and primary agent workflows.
   REPAIR      Repair planning and persisted repair-history analysis.
   TOOLS       Built-in tools, MCP, language guidance, and parity reports.
   GITHUB      Guarded GitHub and local Git-context commands.
   SESSIONS    Persistent session and transcript/result commands.
   AUTOMATION  Autonomous-loop and AUTOPILOT commands.
   CONTEXT     Project instructions and bounded repository context.
   PATCHING    Structured multi-hunk patch commands.

3 CORE
 General commands and persistent profile/configuration commands.

4 HELP
 Command: HELP
 Performs: Lists the currently registered OVMS Agent commands.
 Use when: You do not remember a command name or want a quick live inventory.

4 VERSION
 Command: VERSION
 Performs: Displays the OVMS Agent version string.
 Use when: Reporting a defect, comparing systems, or verifying the installed image.

4 QUIT
 Command: QUIT
 Performs: Stops OVMS Agent.
 Use when: Leaving the interactive program normally.

4 EXIT
 Command: EXIT
 Performs: Alias for QUIT.
 Use when: EXIT is more natural to you; it has the same effect as QUIT.

4 SETTINGS_CMD
 Command: SETTINGS [SHOW|GET name|SET name value|TOGGLE name|RESET|RELOAD]
 Performs: Views or changes persistent OVMS Agent settings.
 Use when: Configuring normal behavior instead of defining legacy logical names.
 See the SETTINGS topic for every individual setting.

4 MODEL_CMD
 Command: MODEL [model-name]
 Performs: Shows or sets the model of the active AI provider.
 Use when: Staying on the same provider profile but choosing another supported model.

4 PROVIDER_CMD
 Command: PROVIDER [LIST|SHOW [name]|USE name|ADD name|DELETE name]
 Performs: Manages persistent AI provider profiles.
 Use when: Adding a provider, switching endpoints/accounts, inspecting a profile,
 or removing one.  ADD prompts for service address, model, and API key.

4 GITHUB_PROFILE
 Command: GITHUB [LIST|SHOW [name]|USE name|ADD name|DELETE name]
 Performs: Manages persistent GitHub profiles.
 Use when: Configuring or switching the repository/user/branch/token profile used
 by guarded GitHub integration.

4 DCL
 Command: DCL <single DCL command>
 Performs: Runs one DCL command through the full-policy path, captures output,
 records the OpenVMS status, and logs the operation.
 Use when: A direct OpenVMS command is genuinely required and full approval,
 guarded writes, and DCL execution have intentionally been enabled.
 Notes: Commands beginning EXIT, LOGOUT, STOP, GOTO, CALL, RETURN, SUBROUTINE,
 SPAWN, SUBMIT, or PIPE are refused, as are @ command procedures.  This path is
 intentionally more powerful than RUN.

4 RUN
 Command: RUN "command"
 Performs: Runs one narrowly allow-listed read-oriented DCL command after
 confirmation when DCL execution is enabled.
 Use when: You need simple local inspection such as SHOW, DIRECTORY, SEARCH,
 TYPE, DIFFERENCES, or read-only Git status/diff/log/show without broader DCL.
 Notes: RUN refuses known mutating/system verbs, redirection, command procedures,
 command chaining, and other unsafe syntax.

4 CHANGESET
 Command: CHANGESET BEGIN | ADD path "replacement text" | SHOW | APPLY | CANCEL
 Performs: Builds a transactional multi-file change set with confirmation and
 rollback protection.
 Use when: Several exact replacements or creations must succeed as one guarded
 unit instead of leaving a partially applied set of edits.

5 CHANGESET_BEGIN
 Command: CHANGESET BEGIN
 Performs: Starts an empty transactional change set.
 Use when: Preparing several exact file operations that should be applied together.

5 CHANGESET_ADD
 Command: CHANGESET ADD path "replacement text"
 Performs: Adds one replacement or creation to the active change set.
 Use when: Another exact operation belongs in the same all-or-rollback transaction.

5 CHANGESET_SHOW
 Command: CHANGESET SHOW
 Performs: Lists the staged operations.
 Use when: Reviewing the complete transaction before APPLY.

5 CHANGESET_APPLY
 Command: CHANGESET APPLY
 Performs: Confirms and applies the staged operations transactionally, rolling
 completed work back if the full transaction cannot finish.
 Use when: The staged operations are correct and guarded writes are intentionally enabled.

5 CHANGESET_CANCEL
 Command: CHANGESET CANCEL
 Performs: Discards the active change set without applying it.
 Use when: The staged operations are obsolete, incorrect, or no longer needed.

3 PROJECT
 Repository, file, search, build, and direct project-edit commands.

4 ROOT
 Command: ROOT
 Performs: Displays the configured project root.
 Use when: Confirming which tree relative-path commands will operate on.

4 STATUS
 Command: STATUS
 Performs: Displays general project/agent status.
 Use when: Doing a quick environment check or diagnosing root/gate/configuration trouble.

4 LIST
 Command: LIST [path]
 Performs: Lists a project directory using the normal filtered view.
 Use when: Browsing a directory without extra files exposed by LIST/ALL.

4 LIST_ALL
 Command: LIST/ALL [path]
 Performs: Lists a directory using the include-all view.
 Use when: Normal LIST may be hiding files and you need the fullest inventory.

4 TREE
 Command: TREE [path]
 Performs: Displays a bounded project directory tree.
 Use when: Understanding layout before choosing source, test, or build files.

4 READ
 Command: READ file [start [count]]
 Performs: Reads a bounded line range from a project file.
 Use when: You know the file and need exact context without dumping the whole file.

4 BUILD
 Command: BUILD
 Performs: Runs the project BUILD.COM, captures output, and preserves failed
 build output for diagnosis.
 Use when: Validating changes or reproducing a build problem on OpenVMS.

4 GITSTATUS
 Command: GITSTATUS
 Performs: Displays Git working-tree status.
 Use when: Checking for intended and unintended local changes before or after edits.

4 GITDIFF
 Command: GITDIFF
 Performs: Displays uncommitted source changes.
 Use when: Reviewing the exact local patch before accepting or committing work.

4 EDIT
 Command: EDIT file
 Performs: Invokes the guarded interactive edit workflow for one project file.
 Use when: A manual interactive edit is more appropriate than PATCH or an agent change.

4 GREP
 Command: GREP "text" [path] [/CONTEXT=n] [/LIMIT=n] [/CASE=value]
          [/COUNT] [/FILES] [/NAME=pattern] [/EXCLUDE=pattern]
          [/DEPTH=n] [/WORD]
 Performs: Searches project files with bounded recursion and filtering controls.
 Use when: Repository-wide discovery needs more control than single-file SEARCH.
 Notes: CONTEXT is 0-20, LIMIT is 1-100, DEPTH is 0-16, and CASE accepts
 SENSITIVE or INSENSITIVE.  COUNT and FILES are mutually exclusive and cannot
 be combined with nonzero CONTEXT.

4 SEARCH
 Command: SEARCH file "text"
 Performs: Searches one named file for text.
 Use when: The target file is already known and GREP would be unnecessarily broad.

4 PATCH
 Command: PATCH file "old" "new"
 Performs: Replaces one exact text occurrence through the direct patch command.
 Use when: A small deterministic replacement is known exactly.

3 SYMBOLS
 C symbol-index inspection, dependency analysis, and structured refactoring.

4 REINDEX
 Command: REINDEX
 Performs: Rebuilds the persistent C symbol index.
 Use when: Source changed and symbol/dependency queries may be stale.

4 INDEX_STATUS
 Command: INDEX/STATUS
 Performs: Shows symbol-index statistics.
 Use when: Confirming that an index exists and checking its basic scale/health.

4 INDEX_CHECK
 Command: INDEX/CHECK
 Performs: Checks whether the symbol index is current.
 Use when: Files may have changed since the last index and analysis must be trusted.

4 WHERE
 Command: WHERE symbol
 Performs: Finds a C symbol definition.
 Use when: You know an identifier but not where it is defined.

4 CALLERS
 Command: CALLERS symbol
 Performs: Finds C call sites for a symbol.
 Use when: Preparing to change a function interface or behavior.

4 SYMBOL
 Command: SYMBOL symbol
 Performs: Shows definitions and call references together.
 Use when: A compact definition-plus-usage view is more useful than separate queries.

4 SHOW_FUNCTION
 Command: SHOW/FUNCTION symbol
 Performs: Displays a complete C function body.
 Use when: A symbol lookup found the function and you need its full implementation.

4 SHOW_STRUCT
 Command: SHOW/STRUCT symbol
 Performs: Displays a struct, union, or enum definition.
 Use when: Reasoning about data layout, fields, or type coupling.

4 SHOW_MODULE
 Command: SHOW/MODULE file.c
 Performs: Summarizes one local C or header module.
 Use when: Understanding a module before editing or moving code.

4 DEPENDENCIES
 Command: DEPENDENCIES file.c
 Performs: Shows local dependencies of a module.
 Use when: Preparing include, module-boundary, or ownership changes.

4 WHO_USES
 Command: WHO/USES symbol
 Performs: Finds local uses of a C identifier.
 Use when: Rename/delete work needs broader usage evidence than call sites alone.

4 MODULE_GRAPH
 Command: MODULE/GRAPH file.c
 Performs: Shows direct project-module dependencies for one module.
 Use when: Inspecting coupling around a particular module.

4 MODULE_GRAPH_ALL
 Command: MODULE/GRAPH/ALL
 Performs: Shows the project-wide module graph.
 Use when: Reviewing architecture or choosing refactoring boundaries.

4 IMPACT
 Command: IMPACT symbol
 Performs: Shows direct change impact for a symbol.
 Use when: Estimating affected code before modifying a shared symbol.

4 MODULE_REVERSE
 Command: MODULE/REVERSE file.c
 Performs: Shows modules that depend on a module.
 Use when: Considering changes or removal and you need reverse dependencies.

4 PATH
 Command: PATH source target
 Performs: Finds a dependency path between two endpoints.
 Use when: Understanding how apparently distant components are connected.

4 CLUSTER
 Command: CLUSTER
 Performs: Identifies strongly connected module groups.
 Use when: Finding tightly coupled areas that may need to be refactored together.

4 CYCLE
 Command: CYCLE
 Performs: Shows a project dependency cycle.
 Use when: Diagnosing circular architecture or include/build coupling.

4 CYCLE_MODULE
 Command: CYCLE/MODULE file.c
 Performs: Shows a dependency cycle reachable from one module.
 Use when: A particular module is suspected of participating in a cycle.

4 ARCHITECTURE
 Command: ARCHITECTURE
 Performs: Summarizes project architecture metrics.
 Use when: Doing a high-level structural health review before a larger refactor.

4 UNUSED
 Command: UNUSED
 Performs: Finds likely unused functions and modules.
 Use when: Identifying cleanup candidates.  Treat results as evidence to review,
 not automatic deletion authority.

4 UNUSED_DETAIL
 Command: UNUSED/DETAIL symbol
 Performs: Explains unused-code evidence for one symbol.
 Use when: UNUSED identifies a candidate and you want supporting evidence first.

4 RENAME
 Command: RENAME old_name new_name
 Performs: Previews an exact project-wide identifier rename.
 Use when: Renaming a C identifier; preview first so all proposed edits can be reviewed.

4 RENAME_APPLY
 Command: RENAME/APPLY old_name new_name
 Performs: Applies a confirmed project-wide identifier rename.
 Use when: The RENAME preview is correct and write prerequisites are satisfied.

4 RENAME_VERIFY
 Command: RENAME/VERIFY old_name new_name
 Performs: Applies a rename, reindexes, builds, and rolls back on failure.
 Use when: You want rename execution tied directly to verification and rollback.

4 EXTRACT_FUNCTION
 Command: EXTRACT/FUNCTION file.c START:END new_name
 Performs: Previews extracting a source range into a new function.
 Use when: A contiguous block should become a helper and you want to inspect the
 transformation before writing.

4 EXTRACT_FUNCTION_APPLY
 Command: EXTRACT/FUNCTION/APPLY file.c START:END new_name
 Performs: Applies one guarded function extraction.
 Use when: The extraction preview is correct and the selected range is suitable.

4 EXTRACT_FUNCTION_VERIFY
 Command: EXTRACT/FUNCTION/VERIFY file.c START:END new_name
 Performs: Checks extraction semantics without writing.
 Use when: You want stronger validation of a candidate before applying it.

4 INLINE_FUNCTION
 Command: INLINE/FUNCTION symbol
 Performs: Previews inlining a local C function.
 Use when: A small helper may no longer justify a separate function.

4 INLINE_FUNCTION_APPLY
 Command: INLINE/FUNCTION/APPLY symbol
 Performs: Applies one guarded local function inline.
 Use when: The inline preview is satisfactory and the helper is an appropriate candidate.

4 DELETE_FUNCTION
 Command: DELETE/FUNCTION symbol
 Performs: Previews deleting a function that appears unused.
 Use when: UNUSED evidence suggests a safe removal candidate.

4 DELETE_FUNCTION_APPLY
 Command: DELETE/FUNCTION/APPLY symbol
 Performs: Deletes one unused function with verification.
 Use when: The delete preview and unused evidence are satisfactory.

4 MOVE_FUNCTION
 Command: MOVE/FUNCTION symbol destination.c
 Performs: Previews moving a function between modules.
 Use when: Responsibility belongs in another module and dependency effects need review.

4 MOVE_FUNCTION_APPLY
 Command: MOVE/FUNCTION/APPLY symbol destination.c
 Performs: Moves one function between modules with verification.
 Use when: The move preview is correct and module ownership is understood.

4 DECLARATIONS_CHECK
 Command: DECLARATIONS/CHECK [file.c]
 Performs: Audits static-helper declaration order.
 Use when: DEC C reports implicit declarations or C89 declaration order is suspect.

4 DECLARATIONS_FIX
 Command: DECLARATIONS/FIX [file.c]
 Performs: Inserts missing static-helper prototypes.
 Use when: Verified declaration-order issues should be repaired without moving functions.

4 DECLARATIONS_FIX_AUTO
 Command: DECLARATIONS/FIX/AUTO [file.c]
 Performs: Repairs declaration order in verified batches.
 Use when: Several validated declaration-order problems need bounded automatic repair.

4 DECLARATIONS_DIAGNOSE
 Command: DECLARATIONS/DIAGNOSE [file.c]
 Performs: Explains declaration-fix generation.
 Use when: You need to understand why a prototype repair is or is not proposed.

3 LLM
 Direct LLM interaction and primary agent workflows.

4 ASK
 Command: ASK prompt
 Performs: Sends one prompt to the configured LLM without maintaining CHAT context.
 Use when: You have a one-off question or transformation that needs no conversation history.

4 CHAT
 Command: CHAT prompt
 Performs: Continues a stateful LLM conversation.
 Use when: Follow-up questions depend on earlier CHAT context.

4 CHAT_RESET
 Command: CHAT/RESET
 Performs: Clears the current LLM conversation.
 Use when: Earlier CHAT context is no longer relevant or is contaminating a new task.

4 REVIEW
 Command: REVIEW file
 Performs: Asks the configured LLM to review a source file.
 Use when: You want focused code-review feedback without a write workflow.

4 AGENT
 Command: AGENT goal
 Performs: Runs the read-only AI agent against the project.
 Use when: A goal needs tool/file investigation but no project writes.

4 AGENT_WRITE
 Command: AGENT/WRITE goal
 Performs: Runs the guarded write agent.
 Use when: An AI-directed goal legitimately requires project edits and write/policy
 gates are intentionally enabled.
 Notes: M289 adds the guarded build_source tool for one native source file.  It
   loads the matching LANGUAGE/<LANGUAGE>.BUILD profile and executes only when
   full approval, guarded writes, and DCL execution are all enabled.  The first
   native-accepted profile is MACRO32 (.MAR); compile must succeed before link,
   and native status plus captured command output are returned to the agent.

4 AGENT_BUILD
 Command: AGENT/BUILD goal
 Performs: Runs controlled project build analysis.
 Use when: The task centers on building, interpreting failures, and identifying fixes.

4 AGENT_FIX
 Command: AGENT/FIX goal
 Performs: Applies one confirmed fix and builds.
 Use when: A supervised single correction is understood and should be verified immediately.

4 AGENT_RETRY
 Command: AGENT/RETRY goal
 Performs: Retries one supervised fix using prior build diagnostics.
 Use when: The previous supervised fix/build failed and saved diagnostics should guide
 one more bounded attempt.

4 AGENT_SELFTEST
 Command: AGENT/SELFTEST
 Performs: Runs non-destructive agent checks.
 Use when: Verifying installation/configuration or preparing acceptance evidence.

4 AGENT_STATUS
 Command: AGENT/STATUS
 Performs: Shows agent capabilities and recent workflow state.
 Use when: Deciding what is available or diagnosing why a workflow is unavailable.

4 AGENT_VERIFY
 Command: AGENT/VERIFY
 Performs: Runs safety checks and a controlled build.
 Use when: You want both guard checks and build evidence before accepting a tree.

4 AGENT_CREATE
 Command: AGENT/CREATE goal
 Performs: Creates one new confirmed project file through the guarded creation path.
 Use when: The desired result is specifically one new file rather than general editing.

4 AGENT_IMAGE
 Command: AGENT/IMAGE image-path goal
 Performs: Runs a read-only agent with a local image supplied as context.
 Use when: Analysis depends on a screenshot, diagram, scan, or other local visual evidence.

4 AGENT_PLAN
 Command: AGENT/PLAN goal
 Performs: Creates and saves a read-only implementation plan.
 Use when: A nontrivial change should be reviewed separately from execution.

4 AGENT_PLAN_SHOW
 Command: AGENT/PLAN/SHOW
 Performs: Displays the saved implementation plan.
 Use when: Reviewing exactly what has been planned before approval or execution.

4 AGENT_PLAN_CLEAR
 Command: AGENT/PLAN/CLEAR
 Performs: Clears the saved plan after confirmation.
 Use when: The plan is obsolete, incorrect, or should not be executed.

4 AGENT_PLAN_VALIDATE
 Command: AGENT/PLAN/VALIDATE
 Performs: Validates saved-plan file fingerprints.
 Use when: Executing an older plan and target files may have changed underneath it.

4 AGENT_APPROVE
 Command: AGENT/APPROVE
 Performs: Approves the current saved plan for this session.
 Use when: The saved plan has been reviewed and validated and guarded execution is intended.

4 AGENT_EXECUTE_DRY_RUN
 Command: AGENT/EXECUTE/DRY_RUN
 Performs: Validates and stages a saved plan without writing.
 Use when: You want a final no-write proof that the plan is still executable.

4 AGENT_EXECUTE
 Command: AGENT/EXECUTE
 Performs: Executes one validated saved-plan operation.
 Use when: A reviewed/approved plan should be executed step by step rather than free-form.

4 AGENT_APPROVAL
 Command: AGENT/APPROVAL
 Performs: Shows the current agent approval policy.
 Use when: Troubleshooting permission refusals or checking a workflow prerequisite.

4 AGENT_APPROVAL_SET
 Command: AGENT/APPROVAL/SET policy
 Performs: Sets the session approval policy.
 Use when: A temporary session policy change is needed without relying only on SETTINGS.

4 AGENT_APPROVAL_RESET
 Command: AGENT/APPROVAL/RESET
 Performs: Resets the session approval policy.
 Use when: A temporary policy change should be removed.

4 AGENT_CONTEXT
 Command: AGENT/CONTEXT
 Performs: Shows the current agent execution context.
 Use when: Verifying root, policy, provider, and other context the agent is actually using.

4 AGENT_EXEC
 Command: AGENT/EXEC goal
 Performs: Runs a goal using the current approval policy.
 Use when: You want the policy-aware execution framework to choose allowed guarded work.

4 AGENT_EXEC_DRY
 Command: AGENT/EXEC/DRY goal
 Performs: Creates a read-only implementation plan through the execution framework.
 Use when: Previewing what AGENT/EXEC would attempt without making changes.

4 AGENT_EXEC_RESUME
 Command: AGENT/EXEC/RESUME goal
 Performs: Executes a goal in the current persistent session.
 Use when: Continuing a task that should retain current persistent session context.

4 AGENT_EXEC_FORK
 Command: AGENT/EXEC/FORK goal
 Performs: Forks the current session and executes a goal in the fork.
 Use when: Exploring an alternative approach without disturbing the original session line.

4 AGENT_EXEC_RESUME_CREATE
 Command: AGENT/EXEC/RESUME/CREATE goal
 Performs: Resumes persistent context and creates one guarded file.
 Use when: A continuing session specifically needs one new file while retaining context.

4 OBSERVABILITY
 Activity, persistent state, and local history commands.

5 AGENT_LOG
 Command: AGENT/LOG
 Performs: Displays the structured local activity log.
 Use when: Auditing recent agent actions or diagnosing a workflow sequence.

5 AGENT_LOG_OLD
 Command: AGENT/LOG/OLD
 Performs: Displays the rotated activity log.
 Use when: The event you need has rolled out of the active log.

5 AGENT_LOG_CLEAR
 Command: AGENT/LOG/CLEAR
 Performs: Clears the active activity log after confirmation.
 Use when: Intentionally starting a fresh audit window and old evidence is no longer needed.

5 AGENT_METRICS
 Command: AGENT/METRICS
 Performs: Summarizes structured activity metrics.
 Use when: You want aggregate activity rather than the full event log.

5 AGENT_STATE
 Command: AGENT/STATE
 Performs: Displays validated persistent workflow state.
 Use when: Resuming work or diagnosing saved plan/repair/workflow state.

5 AGENT_MEMORY
 Command: AGENT/MEMORY
 Performs: Shows recent stored agent history.
 Use when: Recent durable context is needed without reading the full structured log.

5 AGENT_STATE_CLEAR
 Command: AGENT/STATE/CLEAR
 Performs: Clears persisted workflow state after confirmation.
 Use when: Saved workflow state is intentionally abandoned or known to be unusable.

3 REPAIR
 Supervised repair workflow and persisted repair-history analysis.

4 AGENT_REPAIR
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR goal
 Performs: Runs the dedicated repair workflow for a goal.
 Use when: A build/project failure should be handled by the repair path rather than a
 generic write agent.

4 REPAIR_PLAN
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/PLAN
 Performs: Creates a repair plan from the last failed build.
 Use when: Captured build diagnostics should be turned into an explicit repair plan.

4 REPAIR_STATUS
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/STATUS
 Performs: Summarizes the most recent persisted repair run.
 Use when: You need a quick answer to what happened in the latest repair attempt.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY
 Performs: Shows recent persisted repair runs.
 Use when: Comparing attempts or investigating repeated failures.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_FAILURES
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/FAILURES
 Performs: Shows failed recent repair runs.
 Use when: Success records are noise and only unsuccessful attempts matter.

4 REPAIR_SHOW
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/SHOW plan-hash
 Performs: Shows one persisted repair run by plan hash.
 Use when: A known repair plan must be inspected in detail.

4 REPAIR_EXPORT
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/EXPORT
 Performs: Exports recent repair history to text.
 Use when: Evidence must be archived, shared, or reviewed outside OVMS Agent.

4 REPAIR_STATS
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/STATS
 Performs: Shows aggregate statistics for recent repair runs.
 Use when: Assessing repair reliability across multiple attempts.

4 REPAIR_CONFIG
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/CONFIG
 Performs: Shows resolved repair-history configuration.
 Use when: Retention/location/limits appear wrong or need verification.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_INFO
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/INFO
 Performs: Shows repair-history metadata.
 Use when: Checking the history store itself rather than an individual run.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_CHECK
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/CHECK
 Performs: Checks repair-history integrity.
 Use when: History output looks damaged, incomplete, or inconsistent.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_DIAG
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/DIAG
 Performs: Shows combined repair-history diagnostics.
 Use when: Configuration, integrity, and history evidence are needed together.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_COUNT
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/COUNT
 Performs: Shows concise persisted repair counts.
 Use when: A script or quick diagnostic needs counts rather than full records.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_LATEST
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/LATEST
 Performs: Shows the newest repair run.
 Use when: Only the most recent run matters.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_OLDEST
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/OLDEST
 Performs: Shows the oldest retained repair run.
 Use when: Investigating the beginning of the retained history window.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_OUTCOME
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/OUTCOME outcome
 Performs: Filters recent runs by final outcome.
 Use when: Analyzing only successes, failures, or another supported outcome class.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_ATTEMPTS
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/ATTEMPTS count
 Performs: Filters repair runs by attempt count.
 Use when: Comparing first-try work with retry-heavy repairs.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_PLAN
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/PLAN hash-prefix
 Performs: Filters history by plan-hash prefix.
 Use when: Several records may belong to a known repair plan.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_SINCE
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/SINCE timestamp
 Performs: Filters repair runs by start timestamp.
 Use when: Limiting analysis to a recent incident or acceptance window.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_REPORT
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/REPORT
 Performs: Shows a formatted recent repair report.
 Use when: Human-readable review is preferable to terse raw records.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_SUMMARY
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/SUMMARY
 Performs: Shows a compact recent repair summary.
 Use when: You want an overview before drilling into individual runs.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_CSV
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/CSV
 Performs: Exports recent repair history as CSV.
 Use when: Spreadsheet/statistical analysis or machine processing is desired.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_KV
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/KV
 Performs: Exports repair history as key/value text.
 Use when: Simple machine parsing is easier than CSV.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_COMPARE
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/COMPARE
 Performs: Compares the newest two repair runs.
 Use when: A retry changed behavior and the immediate before/after matters.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_STREAK
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/STREAK
 Performs: Shows the current repair outcome streak.
 Use when: Looking for consecutive successes or repeated failures.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_RECOVERY
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/RECOVERY
 Performs: Summarizes two-attempt repair recovery.
 Use when: Evaluating whether second attempts recover from first-attempt failure.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_TREND
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/TREND
 Performs: Shows recent repair success trend.
 Use when: Checking whether repair reliability is improving or degrading.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_FIRST
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/FIRST n
 Performs: Shows the oldest N repair runs.
 Use when: Sampling the beginning of retained history.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_LAST
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/LAST n
 Performs: Shows the newest N repair runs.
 Use when: Sampling only the most recent activity.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_RANGE
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/RANGE start count
 Performs: Shows a newest-first history slice.
 Use when: Paging through large history in bounded chunks.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_INDEX
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/INDEX n
 Performs: Shows one newest-first indexed repair run.
 Use when: Another history command gives an index and you want that exact record.

4 REPAIR_HISTORY_CLEAR
 Command: AGENT/REPAIR/HISTORY/CLEAR
 Performs: Clears persisted repair history with explicit confirmation.
 Use when: History is intentionally being discarded and is no longer needed for audit.

3 TOOLS
 Built-in/external tools, MCP, language guidance, and parity reporting.

4 AGENT_TOOLS
 Command: AGENT/TOOLS
 Performs: Lists agent tools and safety classifications.
 Use when: Discovering available tools or checking their safety tier before execution.

4 AGENT_TOOLS_EXT
 Command: AGENT/TOOLS/EXT
 Performs: Lists current built-in and external parity tools.
 Use when: Checking which capabilities are native versus integrated externally.

4 AGENT_TOOLS_INFO
 Command: AGENT/TOOLS/INFO tool-name
 Performs: Shows metadata for one tool.
 Use when: You know the tool name and need its policy or capability details.

4 AGENT_TOOL_RUN
 Command: AGENT/TOOL/RUN tool arguments
 Performs: Runs one approved built-in tool directly.
 Use when: Deterministic tool invocation is preferable to model planning.

4 AGENT_TOOL_LAST
 Command: AGENT/TOOL/LAST
 Performs: Shows the last persisted tool invocation.
 Use when: Inspecting the most recent tool evidence after an unexpected result.

4 AGENT_TOOL_HISTORY
 Command: AGENT/TOOL/HISTORY
 Performs: Shows recent persisted tool invocations.
 Use when: Auditing a sequence of direct or model-driven tool calls.

4 AGENT_TOOL_CLEAR
 Command: AGENT/TOOL/CLEAR
 Performs: Clears persisted tool/session transcript history.
 Use when: Stored tool evidence is intentionally being reset.

4 AGENT_MCP
 Command: AGENT/MCP
 Performs: Lists configured MCP tool servers.
 Use when: Discovering external MCP integrations before inspecting or calling one.

4 AGENT_MCP_INFO
 Command: AGENT/MCP/INFO server
 Performs: Shows one configured MCP tool server.
 Use when: Checking one server's configuration or capabilities.

4 AGENT_MCP_CALL
 Command: AGENT/MCP/CALL ...
 Performs: Validates and plans one guarded MCP call.
 Use when: An external operation needs policy validation and review before execution.

4 AGENT_MCP_EXEC
 Command: AGENT/MCP/EXEC ...
 Performs: Executes and records one approved MCP call.
 Use when: The MCP call has been reviewed/approved and external execution is intended.

4 AGENT_LANG
 Command: AGENT/LANG
 Performs: Lists OpenVMS programming-language support and language guidance.
 Use when: Determining whether the agent has specific guidance for a project language.
 Notes: M288 also ships shared, curated packs under LANGUAGE/<LANGUAGE>.MD.
   AI-assisted requests automatically load a matching pack when the task text or
   source extension strongly identifies a language; the current project directory
   name is used as a fallback.  Packs are bounded to 16 KiB.  If no language is
   confidently detected or no matching pack exists, request behavior is unchanged.
   When a pack is loaded, the initial request reports
   "Language knowledge: <language> (<bytes> bytes)."

4 AGENT_LANG_INFO
 Command: AGENT/LANG/INFO language
 Performs: Shows guidance for one OpenVMS programming language.
 Use when: Compiler/platform conventions for that language matter to an edit.

4 AGENT_LANG_DETECT
 Command: AGENT/LANG/DETECT file
 Performs: Detects language from an OpenVMS source filename.
 Use when: The file's extension or naming convention is unfamiliar.

4 AGENT_LANG_POLICY
 Command: AGENT/LANG/POLICY
 Performs: Shows multilingual policy injected into agent context.
 Use when: Diagnosing how language-specific guidance affects agent behavior.
 Notes: Shared M288 language-pack guidance is appended to request instructions;
   it cannot override sandbox, safety, evidence, approval, or write policy.  The
   normal OVMS_AGENT.COM launcher defines OVMS_AGENT_LANGUAGE_DIR to the shipped
   LANGUAGE directory while preserving an explicit preexisting override for
   custom installation or testing.

4 AGENT_PARITY
 Command: AGENT/PARITY
 Performs: Shows legacy Codex-parity capability status.
 Use when: Reviewing historical capability-gap coverage.

4 AGENT_PARITY_FINAL
 Command: AGENT/PARITY/FINAL
 Performs: Shows the final practical Codex-parity report.
 Use when: You want the consolidated parity assessment.

3 GITHUB
 Guarded GitHub integration and local Git-context commands.

4 AGENT_GITHUB
 Command: AGENT/GITHUB
 Performs: Shows guarded GitHub integration and policy.
 Use when: Reviewing supported GitHub actions and their safety requirements.

4 AGENT_GITHUB_STATUS
 Command: AGENT/GITHUB/STATUS
 Performs: Shows local Git working-tree status through the guarded integration.
 Use when: Preparing a GitHub operation and a live local status check is needed.

4 AGENT_GITHUB_REMOTE
 Command: AGENT/GITHUB/REMOTE
 Performs: Shows configured Git remotes.
 Use when: Verifying which repository an upcoming network action will target.

4 AGENT_GITHUB_CHECK
 Command: AGENT/GITHUB/CHECK
 Performs: Checks OpenVMS Git network prerequisites.
 Use when: Authentication, Git/Curl setup, or connectivity may be wrong.

4 AGENT_GITHUB_FETCH
 Command: AGENT/GITHUB/FETCH ...
 Performs: Fetches from a GitHub remote.
 Use when: Updating remote refs without intentionally integrating them into the tree.

4 AGENT_GITHUB_PULL
 Command: AGENT/GITHUB/PULL ...
 Performs: Pulls a GitHub remote/branch through guarded integration.
 Use when: An accepted workflow intentionally needs remote changes integrated locally.

4 AGENT_GITHUB_PUSH
 Command: AGENT/GITHUB/PUSH ...
 Performs: Pushes to a GitHub remote/branch through the guarded path.
 Use when: Local acceptance is complete and publication is explicitly authorized.

4 AGENT_GITHUB_CLONE
 Command: AGENT/GITHUB/CLONE ...
 Performs: Clones a validated GitHub repository.
 Use when: Creating a new local checkout from a known approved repository.

4 AGENT_GITHUB_ISSUES
 Command: AGENT/GITHUB/ISSUES ...
 Performs: Uses the configured GitHub bridge for issue operations.
 Use when: Project tracking requires GitHub issue work through OVMS Agent.

4 AGENT_GITHUB_PR
 Command: AGENT/GITHUB/PR ...
 Performs: Uses the configured GitHub bridge for pull-request operations.
 Use when: Reviewing or managing PR workflow through OVMS Agent.

4 AGENT_GIT_STATUS
 Command: AGENT/GIT/STATUS
 Performs: Shows cached bounded Git working-tree status.
 Use when: Inspecting the Git status that is available to agent context.

4 AGENT_GIT_DIFF
 Command: AGENT/GIT/DIFF
 Performs: Shows cached bounded unstaged Git diff.
 Use when: Inspecting the diff currently available to the agent/model.

4 AGENT_GIT_CHANGED
 Command: AGENT/GIT/CHANGED
 Performs: Shows changed paths from Git status.
 Use when: A concise changed-file inventory is enough.

4 AGENT_GIT_CONTEXT
 Command: AGENT/GIT/CONTEXT
 Performs: Shows Git context injected into agent runs.
 Use when: Diagnosing why the model believes certain changes are present.

4 AGENT_GIT_REFRESH
 Command: AGENT/GIT/REFRESH
 Performs: Refreshes cached Git status and diff.
 Use when: Local files changed and agent-visible Git context is stale.

3 SESSIONS
 Persistent session, transcript, and result-evidence management.

4 SESSION_NEW
 Command: AGENT/SESSION/NEW name
 Performs: Creates and selects a persistent session.
 Use when: Starting a long-running task that should survive process restarts.

4 SESSION_LIST
 Command: AGENT/SESSION/LIST
 Performs: Lists persistent agent sessions.
 Use when: Finding an existing task/session to resume.

4 SESSION_SHOW
 Command: AGENT/SESSION/SHOW [name]
 Performs: Shows one persistent session.
 Use when: Inspecting metadata/state before resuming, forking, renaming, or deleting it.

4 SESSION_RESUME
 Command: AGENT/SESSION/RESUME name
 Performs: Resumes an active persistent session.
 Use when: Continuing previously saved work with its prior context.

4 SESSION_FORK
 Command: AGENT/SESSION/FORK ...
 Performs: Forks a persistent session.
 Use when: Exploring an alternative path while preserving the original history.

4 SESSION_RENAME
 Command: AGENT/SESSION/RENAME ...
 Performs: Renames a persistent session.
 Use when: Its current name no longer describes the task.

4 SESSION_ARCHIVE
 Command: AGENT/SESSION/ARCHIVE ...
 Performs: Archives a persistent session.
 Use when: Work is complete/inactive but history should be retained.

4 SESSION_UNARCHIVE
 Command: AGENT/SESSION/UNARCHIVE ...
 Performs: Unarchives a persistent session.
 Use when: Archived work becomes active again.

4 SESSION_DELETE
 Command: AGENT/SESSION/DELETE ...
 Performs: Deletes a persistent session.
 Use when: The session and retained context are intentionally no longer needed.

4 SESSION_CURRENT
 Command: AGENT/SESSION/CURRENT
 Performs: Shows the current persistent session.
 Use when: Unsure which session subsequent context-aware commands will affect.

4 SESSION_HISTORY
 Command: AGENT/SESSION/HISTORY [name]
 Performs: Shows the persistent transcript for a session.
 Use when: Reviewing conversational/task history before continuing or auditing work.

4 SESSION_RESULTS
 Command: AGENT/SESSION/RESULTS [name]
 Performs: Shows normalized tool evidence for a session.
 Use when: Results/evidence matter more than the conversational transcript.

4 SESSION_RESULTS_LAST
 Command: AGENT/SESSION/RESULTS/LAST [name]
 Performs: Shows the latest normalized tool result for a session.
 Use when: You need only the newest evidence record.

4 SESSION_RESULTS_CLEAR
 Command: AGENT/SESSION/RESULTS/CLEAR ...
 Performs: Clears normalized tool evidence for a session.
 Use when: Stored result evidence is intentionally being reset.

4 SESSION_EXPORT
 Command: AGENT/SESSION/EXPORT ...
 Performs: Exports one persistent session transcript.
 Use when: Offline review, archival, sharing, or milestone evidence is needed.

4 SESSION_EXEC
 Command: AGENT/SESSION/EXEC ...
 Performs: Resumes a session and executes a goal.
 Use when: Continuing a persistent task and immediately running new work in its context.

3 AUTOMATION
 Autonomous-loop status/limits and bounded AUTOPILOT.

4 AUTO_STATUS
 Command: AGENT/AUTO/STATUS
 Performs: Shows last autonomous-loop counters and stop reason.
 Use when: Reviewing how many turns/writes were consumed and why the run stopped.

4 AUTO_LIMITS
 Command: AGENT/AUTO/LIMITS
 Performs: Shows effective autonomous turn/write limits and their value sources.
 Use when: Verifying hard budgets before a run, especially with legacy logical overrides.

4 AUTO_RESET
 Command: AGENT/AUTO/RESET
 Performs: Resets autonomous-loop status counters.
 Use when: Starting a fresh measurement window and previous counters are irrelevant.

4 AUTOPILOT
 Command: AGENT/AUTOPILOT goal
 Performs: Runs the bounded local build/repair/rebuild loop using the established
 guarded workspace safety floor.
 Use when: A well-scoped local repair benefits from repeated plan/fix/build
 iterations with hard turn/write budgets and an explicit terminal state.
 Notes: AUTOPILOT never satisfies FULL approval and cannot automate push,
 GitHub writes, MCP/external operations, or arbitrary DCL.  It stops as
 CLEAN_BUILD, RETRY_BUDGET_EXHAUSTED, or GOAL_GUARD_REJECTED and leaves a
 human-review artifact.

3 CONTEXT
 Restored session context, project instructions, and bounded repository mapping.

4 CONTEXT_SHOW
 Command: AGENT/CONTEXT/SHOW
 Performs: Shows restored context for the current persistent session.
 Use when: Verifying what state was recovered after resume/restart.

4 INSTRUCTIONS
 Command: AGENT/INSTRUCTIONS
 Performs: Shows project-instruction discovery status.
 Use when: Checking whether repository instruction files were found and loaded.

4 INSTRUCTIONS_STATUS
 Command: AGENT/INSTRUCTIONS/STATUS
 Performs: Explicit status form of project-instruction discovery.
 Use when: A script or operator wants the status operation to be unambiguous.

4 INSTRUCTIONS_SHOW
 Command: AGENT/INSTRUCTIONS/SHOW
 Performs: Shows loaded project instructions.
 Use when: Inspecting the exact rules that will constrain agent behavior.

4 INSTRUCTIONS_RELOAD
 Command: AGENT/INSTRUCTIONS/RELOAD
 Performs: Reloads project instructions from disk.
 Use when: Instruction files changed and future agent runs should use them immediately.

4 PROJECT_MAP
 Command: AGENT/PROJECT/MAP
 Performs: Shows a bounded repository map.
 Use when: A compact structural view is needed for human/model context.

4 PROJECT_FILES
 Command: AGENT/PROJECT/FILES
 Performs: Shows a bounded repository file inventory.
 Use when: Discovering files without an unbounded tree dump.

4 PROJECT_SOURCES
 Command: AGENT/PROJECT/SOURCES
 Performs: Shows likely source files.
 Use when: Locating production code while excluding obvious tests/control files.

4 PROJECT_TESTS
 Command: AGENT/PROJECT/TESTS
 Performs: Shows likely regression/test files.
 Use when: Finding validation coverage relevant to a change.

4 PROJECT_BUILD
 Command: AGENT/PROJECT/BUILD
 Performs: Shows likely build/control files.
 Use when: Diagnosing how the project compiles or which procedures drive acceptance.

4 PROJECT_CONTEXT
 Command: AGENT/PROJECT/CONTEXT
 Performs: Shows repository context injected into agent runs.
 Use when: Diagnosing model assumptions about project structure.

4 PROJECT_REFRESH
 Command: AGENT/PROJECT/REFRESH
 Performs: Refreshes the cached repository map.
 Use when: Files were added, removed, or reorganized and context is stale.

3 PATCHING
 Structured multi-hunk agent patch workflow.

4 AGENT_PATCH
 Command: AGENT/PATCH patch-file
 Performs: Applies one prevalidated structured multi-hunk patch file.
 Use when: A reviewed structured patch should be applied through the guarded path.

4 AGENT_PATCH_DRY
 Command: AGENT/PATCH/DRY patch-file
 Performs: Validates a structured patch without writing.
 Use when: Doing the first applicability check before a multi-hunk patch.

4 AGENT_PATCH_VALIDATE
 Command: AGENT/PATCH/VALIDATE patch-file
 Performs: Validates every structured patch hunk without writing.
 Use when: Detailed whole-patch applicability evidence is needed before mutation.

4 AGENT_PATCH_LAST
 Command: AGENT/PATCH/LAST
 Performs: Shows the most recent structured patch result.
 Use when: Inspecting the recorded outcome immediately after validation/application.
